[[@earth-age|Earth formed]] about 4.5 billion years ago. At first it was battered by [[asteroid|asteroids]] and covered in hot, molten rock. Over time it cooled, oceans formed, and the air filled with gases from [[@volcano|volcanoes]], including [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]], water vapour and nitrogen, but almost no oxygen.

Somewhere in this world, simple chemicals began joining into more complex ones. At some point, a few of these were able to copy themselves. Many scientists think an early form of RNA, a close cousin of [[@dna|DNA]], could both carry information and do chemical work, and may have come first. This idea is called the "RNA world".

{{story:dna}}

समुद्रों में हवा से लगभग 50 गुना ज़्यादा कार्बन है। कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड सतह पर समुद्र के पानी में घुल जाती है, ख़ासकर ठंडे पानी में। समुद्र के नन्हे पौधे, जिन्हें फ़ाइटोप्लैंकटन कहते हैं, ज़मीन के पौधों की तरह ही इसे प्रकाश संश्लेषण में इस्तेमाल करते हैं।

जब खोल वाले समुद्री जीव मरते हैं, तो कुछ समुद्र की तली में बैठ जाते हैं। लाखों सालों में उनके खोल ढेर बनकर चूना पत्थर और खड़िया में बदल जाते हैं। इंग्लैंड में डोवर की सफ़ेद चट्टानें ज़्यादातर समुद्र के नन्हे शैवालों के खोलों से बनी हैं। लोगों ने जितनी कार्बन डाइऑक्साइड छोड़ी है, उसका लगभग एक चौथाई समुद्रों ने सोख लिया है। इससे समुद्र का पानी थोड़ा ज़्यादा तेज़ाबी हो गया है, जो मूँगों को नुकसान पहुँचाता है।

{{story:photosynthesis}}

Hail falls most often in central and northern India, in states such as Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Punjab and Haryana, and in the Northeast. In eastern India and Bangladesh, violent spring storms called Nor'westers, or kalbaisakhi in Bengali, often bring hail along with strong winds.

Hills and high plateaus get extra hail. Up there, a falling stone has less warm air to pass through, so it reaches the ground before it melts. During the [[monsoon|monsoon]] itself, hail is rare. The air is so warm and moist that the freezing level sits very high, and most ice melts on the way down.

{{story:clouds-types}}

The ocean moves. Winds push surface currents across the sea, and differences in temperature and saltiness drive slower, deeper ones. Together they form a loop sometimes called the global conveyor belt.

The best known part is the Gulf Stream. It carries warm water from the Gulf of Mexico up the east coast of the USA and across the Atlantic. Along with westerly winds blowing in off the ocean, it helps explain why Britain and Norway are mild for their [[latitude|latitude]]. London lies farther north than Calgary, yet snow there is a rare event. Some scientists worry that melting Arctic ice could slow these Atlantic [[@ocean-currents|currents]], though how much and how soon is still being studied.

{{story:ocean-currents}}

Bees see colour, but not the way [[@eyes-see|your eyes]] do. Human eyes have three kinds of colour [[sensor|sensors]], tuned to red, green and blue light. A honeybee also has three, but they are tuned to green, blue and [[ultraviolet|ultraviolet]]. So bees are poor at seeing red, which to them looks dark, but they see ultraviolet light, which humans cannot. Under a special camera, many flowers show ultraviolet patterns: dark centres, stripes or rings that point towards the nectar. Scientists call them nectar guides.

A sunflower or a marigold looks evenly coloured to us. To a bee, it can look like a bullseye. The pattern says land here, and saves the bee time, which is good for the bee and good for the plant.

{{story:bees-dance}}

Sticking is only half the trick. A gecko that couldn't unstick would be trapped forever. So geckos peel each toe off from the tip, like you'd peel off a sticky note or a plaster. Changing the angle of the hairs makes them let go instantly.

A running gecko attaches and detaches its toes many times every second, taking just a few hundredths of a second to unstick each one. Their feet even clean themselves as they walk. Dirt sticks better to the wall than to the tiny hairs, so it gets left behind with each step.

{{story:robots}}

A kite without a tail often spins and dives. A tail adds a little weight and plenty of drag at the back, which keeps the nose facing into the wind, much as feathers steady an arrow. Box kites and stunt kites are shaped to stay stable without one.

Wind is the other ingredient. Most kites like a breeze of roughly 10–25 km/h, about the speed of a bicycle. Wind is also often steadier higher up, away from trees and buildings, so kites tend to fly better once they climb.

{{story:benjamin-franklin}}

इस्लामी दुनिया में सुलेख सबसे ऊँची कलाओं में से एक बन गया, ख़ासकर [[@quran-intro|क़ुरान]] की नकल उतारने के लिए। बहुत-सी धार्मिक इमारतों में इंसानों के चित्रों की जगह खूबसूरती से लिखी आयतें होती हैं, इसलिए अक्षर खुद सजावट बन गए। फ़ारसी लिखने वालों ने *नस्तालीक़* नाम की एक बहती हुई शैली बनाई, जिसमें [[@urdu|उर्दू]] भी लिखी जाती है।

आगरा का [[@taj-mahal|ताजमहल]] सफ़ेद संगमरमर पर काले पत्थर से जड़ी आयतों से लिपटा हुआ है। इन्हें सुलेखक अमानत ख़ाँ ने बनाया था, जिन्होंने वहीं अपना नाम भी लिखा। बड़े दरवाज़े के [[arch|मेहराबों]] पर अक्षर ऊपर की ओर बड़े होते जाते हैं। ज़मीन से तुम्हारी आँख को वे ऊपर तक एक ही आकार के दिखते हैं।

{{story:scripts-india}}

Psychologists have found that children as young as three or four notice when sweets or stickers are shared unequally, especially if they get less. By around seven or eight, many children will even turn down a bigger share for themselves if it seems unfair to a friend.

That is why "It's not fair" is one of the first arguments children learn to make. It isn't just a complaint. It shows a child is checking whether rules apply to everyone, including adults. Being able to put yourself in someone else's place, called [[@empathy|empathy]], is part of the same skill.

{{story:game-theory}}

Today most countries make laws through elected lawmakers. India's [[@constitution|Constitution]], which came into force on 26 January 1950, is the rule book for how the country is run. It even sets out how the rules themselves can be changed. The United States Constitution was written in 1787 and came into force in 1789.

Smaller rules come from everywhere. Schools have them. Housing societies have them. Your family probably has a few about screen time or who washes the dishes. A rule made by the people it affects is usually easier to follow than one handed down with no explanation.

{{story:traffic-lights}}

The name comes from Bhima, one of the five Pandava brothers in the [[@mahabharata|Mahabharata]]. In the epic, Bhima is famously strong and loves to eat. "Chhota" means small, so the name means "Little Bheem".

{{story:mahabharata}}

Beyond the name and the strength, the cartoon tells its own stories. Bheem lives with ordinary village children, not princes. His adventures are about helping people, outsmarting villains and winning contests, not about the great war of the epic.

India won independence on 15 August 1947, but it still followed laws made under British rule. Over nearly three years, an assembly of Indian leaders wrote a new [[@constitution|Constitution]], with B.R. Ambedkar chairing the committee that drafted it. It came into force on 26 January 1950, and India became a [[republic|republic]].

That date was chosen on purpose. On 26 January 1930 the Indian National Congress had declared Purna Swaraj, complete self-rule, as its goal. Twenty years later the same day became the birthday of the republic.

{{story:constitution}}

On 14 February 1876, Bell's lawyer filed his patent application in Washington, D.C. That same day, Elisha Gray filed a caveat, a notice that he was working on a telephone design and planned to patent it. Which arrived first, and whether anyone shared Gray's ideas with Bell, became the subject of lawsuits for years. Bell won every major court case.

Antonio Meucci, meanwhile, had built voice-carrying devices in the 1850s and 1860s, but he was poor and could not afford to keep up a full patent. In 2002, the US House of Representatives passed a resolution honouring his work on the telephone.

{{story:sound-travel}}

The first cable across the Atlantic, in 1858, carried [[@telegraph|telegraph]] messages in Morse code. It worked for only about three weeks before failing. In 1866 a giant steamship, the Great Eastern, laid a cable that lasted. By 1870 a [[telegraph|telegraph]] line under the sea linked Britain to Bombay, and news that once took weeks to arrive by ship took minutes.

{{story:telegraph}}

The first fibre-optic cable across the Atlantic, called TAT-8, opened in 1988. A single modern cable can carry far more data than all the old telegraph and telephone cables put together.

Three things meet here. First, deep snow covers the valley all winter like a thick blanket, keeping the soil damp and protected. Second, the monsoon reaches this part of the Himalaya and soaks it in July and August. Third, the valley lies between the wet southern hills and the dry lands near Tibet, so plants from both sides grow side by side.

The growing season is short, maybe four months. So every plant is in a hurry to flower, attract [[@pollinators|pollinators]] such as bees and butterflies, and make seeds before the cold comes back. That rush is what makes the meadows explode with colour.

{{story:flowers-colours}}

In 1959, a revolution in Cuba brought Fidel Castro to power. Over the next years, hundreds of thousands of Cubans fled to the USA, and many settled in Miami, only about 370 km from Havana across the sea.

The neighbourhood where many settled became known as Little Havana. On its main street, Calle Ocho, old men play dominoes in a park, cafés sell tiny cups of very sweet, very strong coffee, and cigar makers roll tobacco by hand. Later waves of [[@immigration-stories|immigrants]] from across Latin America and the Caribbean have made Miami a meeting point for the whole region.

{{story:sea-level-rise}}

Much of Siberia sits on [[permafrost|permafrost]], ground that has stayed frozen for thousands of years. Buildings in some cities stand on stilts, so the heat from inside does not melt the ground and make them sink.

Permafrost is also a natural freezer. Woolly [[@mammoths|mammoths]], woolly rhinos and even ancient wolves and lions have been found there with skin, hair and sometimes stomach contents still preserved. As the [[climate|climate]] warms and the ground thaws, more of these frozen animals are turning up.

{{story:sputnik}}

About 550 million years ago, rivers washed sand and pebbles off old mountains into a basin. Over time the sand was buried, squeezed and cemented into a rock called arkose, a kind of sandstone full of feldspar crystals. Then the Earth's crust buckled. The layers that had been lying flat were tipped up until they stood almost straight up.

Softer rock around Uluru wore away over millions of years. The hard arkose stayed. You can still see the tilted layers as stripes running down its sides. Most of the formation is hidden underground, like the bottom of an iceberg.

{{story:rocks-types}}

In 1959, the Dalai Lama left Tibet for India, and tens of thousands of Tibetans followed as [[@refugees|refugees]]. They settled in Dharamshala, in Karnataka, in Delhi's Majnu ka Tilla and in many other places. To earn a living, many cooked the food they knew best.

At around the same time, families from Nepal, [[@sikkim|Sikkim]] and the Northeast were moving to big cities for work and study. Students from Delhi University and office workers in Kolkata discovered momos at small stalls. By the 1990s, momo carts were common on Delhi's streets. A plate was cheap, filling and ready in minutes, and for students living on pocket money, that was hard to beat.

{{story:refugees}}

In the early 20th century, immigrants from Lebanon settled in Mexico. They brought shawarma, meat stacked on a turning vertical spit. Mexican cooks used pork instead of lamb, marinated it with dried chillies and pineapple, and served it in small corn tortillas. The result was tacos al pastor.

Today, tacos are sold from carts on almost every corner of Mexico City. A cook slices meat straight off the spit and flicks a piece of pineapple onto each taco.

{{story:people-street-vendors}}
